{
  "assessment": "The draft establishes the concurrency and deployment reasons to favor managed PostgreSQL for conventional hosted, multi-tenant SaaS, but does not yet state a clear recommendation or adequately answer migration costs. SQLite recovery options and application-development tradeoffs need targeted evidence; the existing pricing comparisons are too weak to support precise cost claims.",
  "rubric": {
    "coverage": 3,
    "support": 3,
    "balance": 3
  },
  "enoughEvidence": false,
  "gaps": [
    {
      "question": "Which documented SQLite-to-PostgreSQL differences make migration costly, and which application requirements favor PostgreSQL from the outset?",
      "angle": "Official SQLite type/SQL compatibility documentation and documented migration limitations",
      "why": "Migration risk is an explicit decision criterion currently supported only by inference, and application requirements could strengthen or qualify the default recommendation."
    },
    {
      "question": "What backup, point-in-time recovery, and failover capabilities can a single-host SQLite SaaS obtain through tools such as Litestream, and what operational work remains?",
      "angle": "Official SQLite replication and recovery tooling documentation",
      "why": "This determines when SQLite remains a credible low-burden alternative rather than unfairly comparing bare SQLite with managed, highly available PostgreSQL."
    }
  ],
  "unsupportedBlockIds": [
    "b15",
    "b16"
  ],
  "conflict": null,
  "decisions": [
    "Official SQLite deployment guidance (s1, s5) and current PostgreSQL concurrency documentation (s6) adequately support the core topology distinction.",
    "Do not replace the website traffic estimate with a universal write-rate threshold: transaction duration, contention, hardware, and latency requirements require workload-specific testing.",
    "The SOC 2 claim in b16 is unsupported; certification alone does not prescribe PostgreSQL or a particular availability architecture.",
    "Treat per-tenant migration and backup burdens in b14 as architectural implications, not measured production outcomes.",
    "Avoid precise pricing conclusions from s16–s20; duplicated comparisons are not independent evidence, and entry tiers are not equivalent to HA deployments.",
    "No further general concurrency research is necessary; the remaining searches should address migration and SQLite recovery."
  ],
  "nextAction": "Use one search for documented SQLite migration/type and application-feature constraints, and one for official Litestream recovery and failover limitations. Then write an explicit managed-PostgreSQL default recommendation with bounded SQLite exceptions, separating backups from HA and qualifying costs and migration effort."
}
